= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The space $W_0^{1,2}(\Omega)$ is the closure of $C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ in the <Sobolev norm>; membership imposes the homogeneous Dirichlet condition in the weak sense, without assuming that the boundary is smooth. A <weak solution> is an element of that space satisfying
$$
\boxed{\int_\Omega a_{ij}D_i uD_j\phi\,dx=-\int_\Omega f\phi\,dx\quad\text{for every }\phi\in C_c^\infty(\Omega).}
$$
Repeated indices are summed. The minus sign comes from integration by parts in the divergence equation. Boundedness of the coefficients, <Hölder's inequality> and density extend this identity to every $\phi\in W_0^{1,2}(\Omega)$. Symmetry permits interchanging the derivative indices in the left side.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose an upper ellipticity bound $\Lambda<\infty$ for the symmetric coefficient matrix, possible because its entries are bounded. Then
$$
\lambda\|Du\|_2^2\leq Q(u,u)\leq\Lambda\|Du\|_2^2.
$$
The <Poincare inequality> on a bounded open set for zero-boundary functions states $\|u\|_2\leq C_P\|Du\|_2$. Consequently
$$
\frac\lambda{1+C_P^2}\|u\|_{W^{1,2}}^2\leq Q(u,u)\leq\Lambda\|u\|_{W^{1,2}}^2.
$$
The symmetric positive <bilinear form> $Q$ is therefore an <inner product>, and its square-root norm is equivalent to the usual <Sobolev norm>. Its triangle inequality follows from the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> for that <inner product>. <completeness> of $W_0^{1,2}$ in its original norm makes it a <Hilbert space> in the $Q$ norm as well.

The linear functional $\ell(v)=-\int fv$ is bounded, since
$$
|\ell(v)|\leq\|f\|_2\|v\|_2\leq C_P\lambda^{-1/2}\|f\|_2 Q(v,v)^{1/2}.
$$
The Hilbert-space <Riesz representation theorem> states that every <bounded linear functional> is the <inner product> with a unique element. Applied in the $Q$ <inner product> it gives a unique $u$ with $Q(u,v)=\ell(v)$ for all $v$, which is precisely the weak-solution identity. Alternatively uniqueness follows directly by testing the difference of two solutions with itself: $Q(u_1-u_2,u_1-u_2)=0$. Thus $\boxed{\text{the zero-boundary problem has exactly one weak solution}}$.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The required functional, with the sign appropriate to a positive divergence operator, is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal F(u)=\frac12Q(u,u)+\int_\Omega fu\,dx.}
$$
It is differentiable on $W_0^{1,2}$, with $D\mathcal F(u)[v]=Q(u,v)+\int fv$. A critical point consequently satisfies the weak equation. We now prove existence by the <direct method in the calculus of variations>, rather than invoke the representation theorem again.

The ellipticity and Poincare estimates imply
$$
\mathcal F(u)\geq\frac\lambda2\|Du\|_2^2-C_P\|f\|_2\|Du\|_2
\geq\frac\lambda4\|Du\|_2^2-\frac{C_P^2}\lambda\|f\|_2^2.
$$
Thus the infimum is finite and any <minimizing sequence> is bounded in the <Sobolev norm>. A bounded sequence in a <Hilbert space> has a <weakly convergent> subsequence; write $u_j\rightharpoonup u$ in $W_0^{1,2}$. The equivalent $Q$ norm has the same continuous linear functionals and hence the same <weak convergence>. A Hilbert norm is <Weak lower semicontinuity>, giving $Q(u,u)\leq\liminf_jQ(u_j,u_j)$. The term $\int fu_j$ converges to $\int fu$ because it is a <bounded linear functional>. Therefore
$$
\mathcal F(u)\leq\liminf_j\mathcal F(u_j)=\inf\mathcal F.
$$
The limit attains the infimum. Differentiating $t\mapsto\mathcal F(u+tv)$ at its minimum, for every $v$, gives $D\mathcal F(u)[v]=0$. This produces the desired <weak solution>. Strict positivity of $Q$ also makes the functional <strictly convex>, consistent with uniqueness.

= 3
